earth: at the animal shelter where you help on Saturdays, a big frightened dog snaps at everyone who comes near
tribal: a wild-caught dog tied by the camp snaps at every hand, and the hunters say to let it go
magic: a beast brought to the guild's yard bites every keeper, and the masters say to send it back
scene: At the shelter where {N} helps on Saturdays there is a big frightened dog that snaps at everyone who comes near the pen. The staff have started talking about giving up on it.
scene W: The shelter has rules for dogs like this, and {N} has been told them twice.
scene U: {N} has noticed that the dog only snaps from one side, and wonders why.
scene B: {N} would like to be the one who turns this dog round, and to be seen doing it.
scene R: {N} thinks the dog just needs to run, hard and far, away from the pens.
scene G: {N} sits by the wire with a hand flat on the floor, and the dog watches.
- W | keep to the shelter's rules and keep a safe distance from the pen | v: conformity, security | foot: lesson rules keep both sides safe | chance: 82
- U | read everything on frightened dogs and try one method patiently | v: self-direction | foot: mark learned a skill | chance: 62
- B | ask the manager for the dog as a project of one's own, and the credit | v: achievement, power | foot: person boss | chance: 55
- R | take the dog for a long run on the beach, off the lead | v: stimulation | foot: mark took a wild risk | chance: 40
- G | sit quietly in the pen each week until the dog comes over on its own | v: universalism, benevolence | dream: test | foot: means time-.05 | chance: 60
- W>B | draw up a training plan for the staff and get it signed off | v: conformity, achievement | foot: person colleague | chance: 58
- U>R | try something new each visit, a toy, a smell, a song, until one works | v: stimulation, self-direction | foot: lesson one small thing changes it | chance: 60
- B>G | pay for the dog's food out of pocket, so it gets more time | v: benevolence | foot: means money-.03 | chance: 80
- R>W | argue the manager out of giving up on the dog | v: benevolence | foot: mark defied an authority | chance: 54
- G>U | learn the dog's moods from the old kennel hand, sign by sign | v: universalism, self-direction | dream: test | foot: person mentor | chance: 66
works: One Saturday the dog walks across the pen and leans its whole weight against {Ns} legs.
works: The staff start asking {N} what to do with the difficult ones, and the dog goes to a good home.
fails: The dog snaps once more, at a child on an open day, and the decision is taken out of everyone's hands.
fails: Months go by and nothing changes, and {N} starts to dread the Saturday visits.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- spark: to know where we come from (G U; cycle s3)

== a box of photographs nobody can name
tier: life event
aim: to know where we come from
aim_role: spark
stages: juvenile young_adult adult mature elder
age: 14-92
per_year: 0 .3 .3 .4 .5 .5
times: while the aim is set, a find from the family's past that nobody can explain comes once or twice (estimate)
tone: mixed
life: family, learning
horizon: months
roles: grandparent, elder, parent, a record keeper
earth: after your grandparent's house is cleared you end up with a shoebox of old photographs with no names on the back
tribal: after the old one's death you are given her bag of carved tokens, each one a person nobody now can name
magic: after the old steward's death you are given his box of small painted likenesses, none of them named
scene: After {Ns} grandparent's house is cleared, {N} ends up with a shoebox of old photographs: weddings, a harbour, a child in a hat too big for it, and not one name on the back.
scene W: {N} wants them sorted, dated and kept properly before anything else is lost.
scene U: {N} keeps coming back to the harbour, and the name of a boat just readable on its side.
scene B: {N} suspects there is a story in the box that the family would rather not tell.
scene R: {N} looks at the faces and feels, oddly, that they are looking back.
scene G: These are {Ns} own people, and it seems wrong that nobody can say who they were.
- W | sort them by decade and label every one that can be dated | v: conformity, tradition | foot: means time-.04 | chance: 74
- U | take them to the town archive and look up the harbour and the dates | v: self-direction | foot: place learn.keeping | chance: 62
- B | post them online and ask who recognises a face | v: achievement | foot: person go-between | chance: 55
- R | put the box away; old faces are no business of the living | v: hedonism, self-direction | dream: drop | foot: means ties-.03 | chance: 90
- G | take them round to the oldest relatives one by one, and listen | v: tradition, benevolence | dream: test | foot: person elder | chance: 70
- W>R | frame the best ones and hang them in the hall for all to see | v: tradition, stimulation | foot: means money-.03 | chance: 80
- U>G | piece the faces into a family tree and fill in the gaps | v: universalism, tradition | dream: test | foot: mark learned a skill | chance: 58
- B>W | write to the harbour town's record office for the old registers | v: security, conformity | foot: person keeper | chance: 60
- R>U | travel to the harbour in the photograph and stand where it was taken | v: stimulation, self-direction | foot: lesson places outlast the people in them | chance: 64
- G>B | ask the family to chip in for a proper album, one copy each | v: tradition, security | foot: means ties+.04 | chance: 68
works: An old cousin names the child in the hat at once, and then talks for two hours about the harbour.
works: The boat's name turns up in a register, and with it a whole branch of the family nobody knew.
fails: The oldest relative who might have known has lost the names, and the faces stay faces.
fails: The trail goes cold at a fire in the records office, years ago, and the box goes back in the cupboard.

